<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/expert/biochemistry-bridge">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Biochemistry is chemistry under life's constraints: water-based, room-temperature, and
    utterly precise. Life doesn't use exotic elements — it uses <strong>ordinary reactions
    orchestrated by enzymes</strong>, compartmentalised into pathways, and powered by one
    universal currency (ATP). The bridge from chemistry to biology is the realisation that a
    cell is a reaction network, and the network can be read.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="respiration — combustion converted into a four-stage installment plan" widget="equationStep" config={biochemCfg} />

  <h2>The four molecule families</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Proteins:</strong> chains of 20 amino acids folded into machines — enzymes (catalysts), motors, channels, antibodies. Function follows 3-D fold; misfolds cause diseases (prions, Alzheimer's).</li>
    <li><strong>Nucleic acids:</strong> DNA stores information in a 4-letter alphabet; RNA copies, edits and regulates. The central dogma: DNA → RNA → protein.</li>
    <li><strong>Carbohydrates:</strong> quick fuel (glucose), storage (glycogen, starch) and structure (cellulose); cell-surface sugars are the ID badges your immune system reads.</li>
    <li><strong>Lipids:</strong> the membrane fabric (a two-layer oil sheet), dense fuel stores, and signalling molecules — hormones, vitamins.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The enzyme miracle, quantified</h2>
  <p>
    Uncatalysed, many biological reactions would take centuries; enzymes cut that to milliseconds
    — speed-ups of up to 10¹⁷× — by gripping substrates in precisely strained orientations. That
    grip is why life runs at 37 °C instead of needing a flame, and why drugs work: most are
    enzyme grips, occupied.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Lehninger, Principles of Biochemistry; Boyer, The Enzymes; Berg, Tymoczko & Stryer.</p>
</TopicPage>
